---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import { guideBySlug } from '../../data/site';
import { CURRENT_TAX_YEAR as YEAR } from '../../data/tax-years';
import { calculateIncomeTax } from '../../lib/income-tax';

const guide = guideBySlug('the-100k-tax-trap');
const gbp = (n: number) => `£${Math.round(n).toLocaleString('en-GB')}`;

// Every figure below is computed by the same engine the calculators use.
const at = (salary: number, region: 'england-nireland' | 'scotland' = 'england-nireland') =>
  calculateIncomeTax({ grossIncome: salary, region }, YEAR);

const marginalRuk = (at(110_001).total - at(110_000).total) * 100;
const marginalScot = (at(110_001, 'scotland').total - at(110_000, 'scotland').total) * 100;

const example = { salary: 110_000, contribution: 10_000 };
const without = at(example.salary);
const withPension = calculateIncomeTax(
  {
    grossIncome: example.salary,
    region: 'england-nireland',
    deductibleFromGross: example.contribution,
  },
  YEAR,
);
const taxSaved = without.total - withPension.total;
---

<GuideLayout guide={guide} sourceKey="payeAndNi">
  <p class="tool-standfirst">
    Between {gbp(100_000)} and {gbp(125_140)} the UK income tax system charges an effective{' '}
    {marginalRuk.toFixed(0)}% — higher than the 45% additional rate that applies above it. Here is
    why, and what to do about it.
  </p>

  <h2>The mechanism</h2>
  <p>
    Everyone starts with a personal allowance of {gbp(YEAR.incomeTax.personalAllowance)} — income
    you pay no tax on. Above {gbp(YEAR.incomeTax.taperThreshold)} of adjusted net income, that
    allowance is withdrawn at <strong>£1 for every £2</strong> you earn.
  </p>
  <p>So earning an extra £100 in this range does two things at once:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The £100 itself is taxed at the higher rate of 40% — that is £40.</li>
    <li>
      You lose £50 of personal allowance. That £50, previously untaxed, is now taxed at 40% — a
      further £20.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    £60 of tax on £100 of extra earnings. An effective marginal rate of{' '}
    <strong>{marginalRuk.toFixed(0)}%</strong>, sustained across the whole{' '}
    {gbp(125_140 - 100_000)} band until the allowance reaches zero at {gbp(125_140)}.
  </p>

  <h2>It is worse in Scotland</h2>
  <p>
    Scottish taxpayers in this range pay the 45% advanced rate rather than 40%, so the same
    mechanism produces an effective marginal rate of{' '}
    <strong>{marginalScot.toFixed(1)}%</strong> — the highest rate anywhere in the UK tax system.
  </p>

  <h2>The absurd bit</h2>
  <p>
    Above {gbp(125_140)} the allowance is gone, so the trap ends and the marginal rate{' '}
    <em>falls</em> to the 45% additional rate. Someone on {gbp(130_000)} faces a lower marginal rate
    than someone on {gbp(110_000)}. A pay rise from {gbp(99_000)} to {gbp(105_000)} is worth
    considerably less after tax than the same rise from {gbp(130_000)} to {gbp(136_000)}.
  </p>

  <h2>What actually helps</h2>

  <h3>Pension contributions</h3>
  <p>
    The trap runs in reverse. A pension contribution reduces your adjusted net income, restoring
    allowance as it goes — so relief comes at the same {marginalRuk.toFixed(0)}%.
  </p>
  <p>
    On a {gbp(example.salary)} salary, a {gbp(example.contribution)} contribution takes adjusted net
    income to exactly {gbp(100_000)}. Your personal allowance goes from{' '}
    {gbp(without.personalAllowance)} back to the full {gbp(withPension.personalAllowance)}, and your
    income tax bill falls by <strong>{gbp(taxSaved)}</strong>. That is{' '}
    {((taxSaved / example.contribution) * 100).toFixed(0)}% relief on money you keep — it has gone
    into your pension, not to HMRC.
  </p>
  <p>
    All three pension arrangements achieve this, because all three reduce adjusted net income. Under
    relief at source you must claim the extra through Self Assessment; it does not happen
    automatically.
  </p>

  <h3>Salary sacrifice on a bonus</h3>
  <p>
    If a bonus is what pushes you over {gbp(100_000)}, sacrificing it into your pension before it is
    paid avoids the trap entirely, and saves National Insurance as well — the only pension
    arrangement that does.
  </p>

  <h3>Gift Aid donations</h3>
  <p>
    Charitable donations under Gift Aid also reduce adjusted net income, extending your basic rate
    band by the grossed-up amount. The mechanism is the same as relief at source.
  </p>

  <h3>The childcare cliff</h3>
  <p>
    If you have young children the trap is sharper still. Tax-Free Childcare and the 30 free hours
    are both lost outright once <em>either</em> parent's adjusted net income exceeds{' '}
    {gbp(100_000)}. There is no taper — it is a cliff. For a family with two children in nursery
    this can be worth several thousand pounds a year, which means the true effective rate just over{' '}
    {gbp(100_000)} can exceed 100%. Earning more genuinely leaves some families worse off.
  </p>
  <p>
    A pension contribution that brings adjusted net income back below {gbp(100_000)} restores both.
    It is one of the few places in the tax system where the arithmetic is this stark.
  </p>

  <h2>What counts as adjusted net income</h2>
  <p>
    Not just salary. It includes bonuses, taxable benefits in kind such as a company car, rental
    profit, dividends and savings interest — then subtracts gross pension contributions and Gift Aid
    donations. It is the pension and Gift Aid deductions that give you a lever.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <strong>Check before the tax year ends</strong>
    <p>
      A pension contribution only affects the year it is paid in. If you are heading into the trap,
      the time to act is before 5 April — not when you file your return the following January.
    </p>
  </div>
</GuideLayout>
